Issue #119: Establish stable pilot production lane and first useful builds

## Founder decision

Start using the Autobuilder for useful production before autonomous refill, automatic merge, and autonomous self-installation are fully accepted.

Use the existing `DanielTabakman/msos-autobuilder` repository as the single source of truth. Do **not** create a second production or development Autobuilder repository.

Separate environments, not source history:

- one exact-commit **stable pilot runtime** that performs approved work;
- one separate **development/staging environment** for candidate Autobuilder versions;
- normal branches and draft PRs in this repository for all source changes.

The stable runtime may build candidate improvements to the Autobuilder, but it must not edit, replace, restart, merge, or install over its own live runtime.

## Why this matters

The core approved-job pipeline already has real evidence for one-shot dispatch, execution, result relay, candidate validation, bounded revision, and controlled draft publication. The remaining Issue #50 and Issue #32 work is concentrated around unattended continuation, Windows runtime handoff, release installation, rollback, and zero-founder-touch operation.

Those later autonomy layers should continue to improve, but they should no longer prevent the accepted existing pipeline from producing useful draft PRs.

## Relationship to existing work

- Issue #50 continues to own automatic capacity-one refill and the final fresh A -> exactly one B -> pause/no-C witness.
- Issue #32 continues to own autonomous self-update, rollback, and zero-founder-touch installation acceptance.
- PR #118 continues to own only the policy-paused stable-bootstrap handoff correction. Its current exact head must be independently reviewed at execution time; this issue does not accept, merge, install, or activate PR #118.
- Issue #73 / merged PR #114 authority remains inactive until its separate runtime requirements are met.
- Issue #78 remains the broader founder-visible status surface; a smaller read-only slice may be selected as an early pilot job only through a separate bounded task packet.

This pilot is deliberately allowed to operate with refill paused and manually initiated one-shot jobs.

## Goal

Prove that one stable Autobuilder version can repeatedly turn founder-approved, bounded tasks into tested draft PRs while Autobuilder development continues safely in a separate lane.

## Required sequence

### 1. Resolve the stable baseline

1. Verify current `main`, open PRs, installed release, stable supervisor, policy, service witnesses, and runtime roots directly.
2. Independently dispose of PR #118 under its own review rules before relying on it.
3. Select and record one exact reviewed commit as the pilot stable source baseline.
4. Do not silently include an unreviewed PR head or mutable branch tip.

### 2. Establish the isolated pilot runtime

Create or designate one non-overlapping runtime root for the stable pilot.

Requirements:

- pinned to the recorded exact commit;
- isolated queue, feed, ledger, lifecycle, marker, release-pointer, temp, workspace, and service state;
- no reuse or mutation of the preserved Issue #50 runtime state;
- copy/import only explicitly allowlisted configuration and credentials required to operate;
- do not copy stale jobs, refill generations, locks, prepared dispatches, service-error markers, workspaces, release pointers, or historical mutable runtime state;
- refill remains paused with `enabled=false` and `desired_capacity=0`;
- no automatic product merge;
- no autonomous Autobuilder installation;
- no live source editing;
- retain an exact rollback target and immutable setup report.

The current Issue #50 runtime and its evidence remain preserved and auditable. This issue must not normalize or destroy it merely to create the pilot.

### 3. Prove the manually initiated production path

Use the accepted one-shot dispatch path for one job at a time.

The first three useful jobs should be:

1. one small, read-only or low-blast-radius Autobuilder improvement;
2. one small PPE/MSOS product improvement that creates external product value;
3. one bounded improvement selected from friction observed in the first two runs.

Each job must have its own approved GitHub task packet, immutable identity, repository/path authority, validation contract, and merge class.

### 4. Keep candidate development separate

For an Autobuilder self-improvement job:

```text
stable version A
-> creates a normal feature branch
-> implements and tests candidate B
-> publishes a draft PR
-> independent review and exact-head CI
-> deliberate promotion decision
-> candidate B is staged separately
-> manual controlled installation initially
-> version A remains available for rollback
```

The producer may not be the sole authority accepting or installing its own candidate.

## Pilot acceptance criteria

- [ ] exact stable commit recorded;
- [ ] stable pilot root is separate from the preserved Issue #50 runtime;
- [ ] refill remains policy-paused throughout initial pilot operation;
- [ ] no autonomous self-installation or automatic merge occurs;
- [ ] immutable setup/configuration report records roots, source commit, release identity, services, policy, and rollback target;
- [ ] first bounded Autobuilder-improvement job reaches a useful tested draft PR;
- [ ] first bounded PPE/MSOS job reaches a useful tested draft PR;
- [ ] third bounded job reaches a useful tested draft PR and addresses observed production friction;
- [ ] all three jobs preserve single-writer/path-authority boundaries;
- [ ] failures are recorded honestly and do not mutate the preserved Issue #50 witness state;
- [ ] a founder-facing pilot recap states time saved, interventions required, failure points, and the next manual step to automate;
- [ ] no new Autobuilder repository is created.

## Stopping rules

Stop and report before mutating runtime when any of the following is unresolved:

- exact stable commit is not independently accepted;
- pilot paths overlap t

