A maintainer on the project eigenius/eigenius (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #119: The ancestor-edge pass in merge conflict detection is unreachable

## What is wrong

`build_merged_subclass_graph` assembles the candidate merged `subclass_of` graph that `detect_inheritance_cycles` walks. After folding in both branches' contributions it runs a second pass whose stated job is to pull in ancestor-defined `subclass_of` edges for classes referenced as a parent but contributed by neither branch. That pass never executes. The set it iterates, `contributed`, is built as the union of `sources_a.keys()` and `sources_b.keys()`; the loop body is then guarded by `only_in_ancestor`, which is true only when the IRI is in neither map. By construction every element of `contributed` is in at least one map, so the guard skips all of them and the body is dead code.

## Where

`kernel/src/layer/merge/conflict.rs:527-550` at `92ffc09`:

```rust
let mut contributed: BTreeSet<Iri> = BTreeSet::new();
contributed.extend(span.sources_a.keys().cloned());
contributed.extend(span.sources_b.keys().cloned());
for iri in &contributed {
    let only_in_ancestor =
        !span.sources_a.contains_key(iri) && !span.sources_b.contains_key(iri);
    if !only_in_ancestor {
        continue;
    }
    let ancestor_res = match find_iri_in_chain(&span.ancestor, iri, topology, backend)? {
        Some((_, r)) => r,
        None => continue,
    };
    // ... ancestor-only class — its arrows pass through to merged.
}
```

The function's own doc comment (`:484-487`) states the intended behaviour, and it is the opposite of what runs:

```rust
/// Returns a `child → [parent]` adjacency map covering every Class
/// referenced by either branch's contributions. Ancestor-only classes
/// are excluded unless they appear as a parent of a contributed
/// class; the cycle detection only needs the reachable subgraph.
```

## Why it matters

`detect_inheritance_cycles` is the stage-2 conflict detector — the one merge check that looks at the merged ontology as a graph rather than IRI by IRI. Its input graph contains only classes one branch or the other redefined. An inheritance cycle that closes through a class **neither branch touched** — branch A adds `X subclass_of Y`, branch B adds `Z subclass_of X`, and the ancestor already holds `Y subclass_of Z` — is invisible to it, because `Y`'s and `Z`'s ancestor-defined arrows never enter the graph. The merge is accepted and the resulting chain carries a `subclass_of` cycle. This is precisely the case the dead pass was written for, and it is the case the classifier's per-IRI stage cannot catch, since neither branch disagrees about any single IRI.

## How to verify

By reading. `contributed` is the union of the two key sets; `only_in_ancestor` is the negation of membership in either. Their conjunction is empty for every iteration. `grep -rn "build_merged_subclass_graph" --include=*.rs .` confirms `detect_inheritance_cycles` (`conflict.rs:474`) is the only caller, so nothing else supplies the missing edges.

## Suggested fix

The set to iterate is not `sources_a.keys() ∪ sources_b.keys()` but the **parent IRIs referenced by the edges collected in the first loop that are not themselves keys of `graph`** — i.e. `graph.values().flatten().cloned().collect::<BTreeSet<_>>().difference(&graph.keys().cloned().collect())`. Those are the classes referenced as a parent and contributed by neither branch, which is what the comment describes. Iterating that set makes the existing body correct as written, and the `only_in_ancestor` guard becomes redundant. The walk should probably iterate to a fixed point, since an ancestor-only class's own parents may themselves be ancestor-only.

A regression test wants three classes: one arrow contributed by A, one by B, and one held only in the ancestor, arranged so the union closes a cycle.

