Issue #359: Define a runtime-enforced child-agent policy envelope

## Goal

Define the runtime-enforced policy envelope required before my-pi can execute owned child agents safely.

This is a required safety boundary for any implementation selected by #358. Prompt instructions are not enforcement. Third-party examples include permission bypass, full-workspace access, and full environment inheritance; those defaults are not acceptable for governed my-pi execution.

## Required policy

- explicit tool allowlists by role;
- child environment allowlisting with no blanket process environment passthrough;
- project-trust-aware resource loading;
- canonical cwd/path scope and enforced worktree isolation for mutating parallel work;
- depth-one default and runtime rejection of unauthorised recursive delegation;
- bounded concurrency, runtime, output, tool calls, and shutdown;
- removal of Factory, Team Mode, Harness, approval, and other control-plane mutation tools from children;
- deterministic cancellation and process-tree/session disposal;
- provenance-preserving structured results whose settlement is not treated as task success;
- compatibility with one authoritative Factory contract, harness, and mutating owner.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] A written threat model covers in-process Pi, Pi subprocess, Claude SDK/CLI, and Codex app-server/CLI execution.
- [ ] Runtime tests prove tool, environment, path, trust, recursion, and control-plane restrictions.
- [ ] Untrusted project configuration fails closed.
- [ ] Cancellation cannot leave an owned child or tool process orphaned.
- [ ] A child cannot weaken its contract, acceptance criteria, or approval boundary.
- [ ] Security defaults are consistent for TUI, print, RPC, and SDK callers.


